Why is version control useful? Because it keeps a complete history of a project, so any change can be traced or undone. It also lets several people work on the same code without overwriting each other, and it records who changed what and why.

What is the difference between staging and committing? Staging selects which changes go into the next commit. Commit saves that selection permanently in the history with a message.

When should you make a commit? Everytime I made a logical change. It is better to commit very often so that you can efficienly track yous changes, and if there is a bug, you would know where it came from. Committing after each meaningful step means you can roll back to a working state if something breaks.

What is the difference between git init and git clone? git init creates a brand-new empty repository in an existing folder, with no history and no remote. git clone copies an existing repository from a server, brins all its files and branches and full commit history, and automatically sets it as origin.

Why should you write good commit messages? To make the history more readable, and to better track all the changes you made.

What is the purpose of using branches? You can work on a feature without disturbing the stable code; commits on the branch are invisible to main until you commit. It also lets severla people work on the same work in parallel, on the same repo.

When would you create a new branch instead of working on main? Whenever you are not sure if the work might break down, you should create a new branch. Main should always stay in a working state, since it's what others clone and what gets deployed. You make your changes on a branch, and then merge it into the main. (at the end, you will delete the branch)

